Run a tree cargo run --release --features clp --bin baldes -- \
  vrptw instances/R101.txt --customers 10 --tree --time 60
 --nodes N sets the tree node budget (default 10,000). --json FILE writesvalidated incumbent routes, bounds, statuses, and work counters. Use--no-enumeration to disable both reduced-cost fixing and enumeration. A complete Rust entry point, requiring clp: use baldes::{
    master::{root::RootProblem, tree::{self, TreeOptions}},
    inputs::{Instance, Rounding},
};
use std::time::Duration;

fn main() -> baldes::Result<()> {
    let mut instance = Instance::read("instances/R101.txt")?;
    instance.truncate(10)?;
    let graph = instance.graph(8, Rounding::None)?;
    let problem = RootProblem::set_partitioning(graph, instance.vehicles as f64)?;
    let mut options = TreeOptions::default();
    options.time_limit = Duration::from_secs(60);
    options.root.cg.multiplicity = (instance.customers.len() - 1) as f64;
    let result = tree::solve(problem, options)?;
    println!("{:?}: incumbent={}, bound={}",
        result.status, result.incumbent, result.bound);
    Ok(())
}
 The CLI tree uses Instance::vehicles as its fleet bound. Root-only CLI andInstance::solve_root use the customer count instead. A custom Rust callerchooses the bound in RootProblem::set_partitioning and configures thecolumn-generation multiplicity separately.

Explicit fixed partial solutions The Rust API can fix additional integer quantities of physical elementary routesin a captured node: use baldes::master::root::{self, FixedRoute};

let child = root::inherit_fixed_routes(
    parent_snapshot,
    &[FixedRoute { arcs: route_arc_ids, quantity: 1 }],
    options,
)?;
 Quantities must be positive. Paths are reconstructed and checked against graphresources and the node's pricing constraints. Calls are cumulative: fixing moreroutes in a returned snapshot retains its existing fixed part. Over-covering acustomer or violating a later branch produces an infeasible residual node.Parents and siblings retain their original row bounds, basis and fixed state. Base and inherited active cut rows are shifted by each added route contribution.The fixed elementary part uses full-memory coefficients for rank-one and strongk-path rows; remaining LP columns can retain their valid weaker limited-memorycoefficients. Separation sees fixed routes alongside residual LP routes, and newcut RHS values are shifted once. This also handles capacity/complement and mappedresource-knapsack rows. Remaining fleet multiplicity and zero-arc propagationapply to the residual routes. LP objectives, Lagrangian bounds, incumbents and enumerated MIP outcomes includethe fixed cost. RootOutput::routes and values retain their residual-columnmeaning. fixed_routes and fixed_cost expose the fixed part;RootOutput::route_values() combines it with positive residual route values.Check solver status and artificial values before accepting an LP witness.Enumerated MIP route values already include fixed routes when a witness exists. A fresh fixed-route solve discards inherited cutoff-restricted enumeration andbucket fixing. It also starts with the caller's incumbent cutoff, because aparent incumbent need not satisfy the fixed restriction. Automatic enumerationand MIP gates retain their existing behavior; fixing routes does not force aninteger solve. The API does not choose routes to fix automatically, and currentlyhas no CLI or Python entry point. The tree does not yet provide multiple pricing networks or cut callbacksduring the integer solve. Python exposes root solving; use Rust or the CLIfor the tree. Infeasible means all nodes were closed without a feasibleincumbent, under the selected bound mode and solver tolerances. Optimal uses the configured absolute and relative gap tolerances. The reportedlower bound retains the minimum node bound across open and tolerance-closedsubproblems; closing a node does not replace its lower bound with the incumbent.The final bound can therefore remain below the incumbent after successful gapclosure. If the retained bound cannot justify closure, the status is Incomplete. Node snapshots and inheritance Tree driver Lifecycle tests: fixing carry, sibling isolation,full-memory updates, grandchildren, and failed setup Tree tests: exhaustive route universes, limits,feasible witnesses, and branch/cut preservation cargo test --features clp --test node_lifecycle --test branch_price
 Vehicle-count upper branches reuse the fleet row when its coefficients matchthe branch. Repeated bounds keep the tighter RHS, while branch depth stillcounts every recorded decision. Fixed route quantities reduce that RHS once.A custom fleet row with different coefficients receives a separate branch row. The root master receives explicit root.initial_routes. Child masters inherittheir parent's restricted model and generated column pool. Routes collectedglobally for integer search, including automatic singletons and routes fromother evaluations, do not preload the child's restricted model. Equal-boundchild insertion visits the upward branch before its downward sibling. Completetree trace parity remains open. For normal tree evaluations, a compatible preceding LP can supply numericalstate after its trailing branch columns and rows are removed. Exact orderedmatrix/cost/sense/offset checks precede transfer, and the target node keeps itsown row and column bounds. Other layouts retain the independent parent fork;strong trials and dives remain isolated. Stabilization centers belong to theparent, while a tightened vehicle upper bound sets the remaining routemultiplicity. Warm metadata retains the parent's numerical and certified lower boundsseparately. Stabilization gap gating compares the selected inherited boundwith the preceding artificial-free restricted-LP boundwithin each CG round. This differs from the global integer incumbent used forpruning and enumeration. Before mutating cuts, an artificial-free completed pricing round retains acheckpoint of the master, route registry, cut/pricing state, stabilization andfixing-gap cache. A later pricing timeout that explicitly requests saved-pointrollback restores that checkpoint and preserves any improved incumbent. Childnodes start with a fresh saved-point flag. Unsaved timeouts, label limits andother errors still propagate. Recovery uses the saved pricing bound; theunfinished cut round's restricted LP objective supplies no new certificate.Failed pricing attempts remain included in the recovered node's pricing-callcount and pricing time, while its bound and warm state come from the checkpoint.

Start with capacity and rank-one cuts cargo run --release --features clp --bin baldes -- \
  vrptw instances/R101.txt --customers 25 --capacity-cuts --rank1 --time 60
 A complete Rust example: use baldes::{master::root::RootOptions, inputs::{Instance, Rounding}};

fn main() -> baldes::Result<()> {
    let mut instance = Instance::read("instances/R101.txt")?;
    instance.truncate(25)?;
    let rounding = Rounding::None;
    let capacity = instance.capacity_cut_config(rounding, false);
    let options = RootOptions::default()
        .with_capacity_cuts(capacity)
        .with_rank_one_cuts();
    let result = instance.solve_root(8, rounding, options)?;
    println!("{:?}: root LP={}", result.master.status, result.master.lp);
    Ok(())
}
 Use capacity_cut_config(rounding, true) to attach the instance's ready/duewindows, service times, and travel matrix for Kohl separation. The CLI/Python2-path switch is for VRPTW; CVRP inputs do not supply time windows. Capacity/Kohl separation has priority 3 at the root and 1 below it. It shares apersistent subset pool. Basic cut rows may be retired by cleanup; reported cutcounts can therefore exceed the number of rows still active.

Controls and API Build and enable the optional tree path: cargo run --features clp,highs --bin baldes -- \
  vrptw instances/R101.txt --customers 10 --tree --enumerated-mip
 TreeOptions::enumerated_mip = Some(MipOptions::default()) enables the samepath in Rust. It is disabled by default to preserve the existing tree schedule.A build without highs rejects this request explicitly. Disabling enumerationleaves no complete universe to send to the MIP solver. MipOptions defaults to 2,000,000 nodes, 360,000 seconds, and no objectivecutoff. The tree additionally caps time at 98% of its remaining global budget.These budgets are separate from the restricted-column heuristic'sTreeOptions::mip_node_limit / mip_time_limit. For a generic integer model, the highs feature alone is sufficient. Thiscomplete example builds a two-variable binary master: use baldes::master::{
    solvers::{highs::{self, HighsMaster}, mip::MipOptions},
    LpMaster, RowSense,
};

fn main() -> baldes::Result<()> {
    let mut master = HighsMaster::new()?;
    let row = master.add_row(RowSense::GreaterEqual, 1.5, &[])?;
    for _ in 0..2 {
        let col = master.add_column(1.0, &[(row, 1.0)])?;
        master.set_upper(col, 1.0)?;
    }
    let result = highs::solve_integer(&master, &[0, 1], &MipOptions::default())?;
    println!("{:?}: {:?}", result.status, result.solution);
    Ok(())
}
 This copies every current physical row, coefficient, cost, and upper boundthrough LpMaster. Columns have lower bound zero in this interface. Unlistedcolumns remain continuous. Integer values and all row activities are checkedbefore returning a feasible witness. Coefficients small enough for HiGHS todiscard are rejected explicitly instead of silently changing the matrix. For a routing snapshot, NodeSnapshot::solve_enumerated_mip prepares the fullroute master and applies the end-of-node route-count gate. It returns Noneif enumeration is incomplete or over the limit. This snapshot API requiresclp,highs; the generic highs::solve_integer API needs only highs.

Examples and verification Rust exposes metadata independently of constructing a graph: use baldes::{core::options::Options, pricing::Solver};

fn main() -> baldes::Result<()> {
    println!("{:?}", Options::support("BALDESlabelDominanceStrategy")?);
    let mut solver = Solver::new(3, 1)?;
    solver.set_option("BALDESlabelDominanceStrategy", 1.0)?;
    Ok(())
}
 Python exposes the same support query and runtime setter: from pybaldes import Solver

names = Solver.option_names()
implemented, scope = Solver.option_support("BALDESlabelDominanceStrategy")
solver = Solver(3, 1)
solver.set_option("BALDESlabelDominanceStrategy", 1.0)
 CLI overrides are repeatable: cargo run --release --features clp --bin baldes -- \
  vrptw instances/R101.txt --customers 10 \
  --option BALDESlabelDominanceStrategy=1 --time 60
 OPTION_TABLE is the source of truth for names,types, defaults, and feature-dependent support. It lists only controls supportedin the current build. Keep this document synchronized when adding a consumer.Bool values are numeric zero/one; integer optionsrequire integral values. Live setters also validate operation-specific modesand limits before changing solver state. Option tests cover canonical discovery in Rust/C,input-alias compatibility, failure atomicity, dominance synchronization, andcapacity overridesin fresh/inherited nodes. Run: cargo test --features clp,highs --test options
cargo test --features clp --test options --test cli
 See Build and validation for the full suite. Separate featurebuilds matter because MIP threshold support changes with the enabled backends.

Configure or disable A complete Rust example, requiring clp: use baldes::{master::root::RootOptions, inputs::{Instance, Rounding}};

fn main() -> baldes::Result<()> {
    let mut instance = Instance::read("instances/R101.txt")?;
    instance.truncate(10)?;
    let mut options = RootOptions::default();
    options.cg.smoothing.initial_alpha = 0.5;
    options.cg.smoothing.beta_factor = 0.0;
    options.cg.smoothing.max_updates = 10;
    let result = instance.solve_root(8, Rounding::None, options)?;
    println!("{:?}: root LP={}", result.master.status, result.master.lp);
    Ok(())
}
 Set options.cg.stabilize = false to disable it in Rust. The CLI exposes: cargo run --release --features clp --bin baldes -- \
  vrptw instances/R101.txt --customers 10 --no-stabilization
 Python exposes the enable/disable switch through the public root API: import pybaldes

result = pybaldes.solve_root(
    "instances/R101.txt", customers=10, stabilize=False,
)
print(result.status, result.lp)
 The Python root API does not expose the individual smoothing parameters. An unavailable best pricing solution differs from a zero subgradient. Feedbackcarries that availability explicitly; the adaptive angle retains its precedingvalue when a best solution is unavailable, matching the native early return.Completed CG rounds use the LP objective accepted within convergence tolerancefor subsequent stabilization gap bookkeeping, while the published pricing boundremains conservative. When an unavailable pricing solution accompanies an accepted pending center,the center uses raw LP duals. The saved normalized subgradient remains intact;the fallback RHS vector does not replace the direction or make it valid.Customer-only elementarity IDs and canonical retained-path export orderpreserve ordering through directional enumeration.
